Keep a 4.0 that fits you well. Upgrade if its lumbar pad bothers you or its hook-and-loop pockets have worn out. First-time buyers should get the 5.0, or a discounted 4.0 if saving money matters more.

The Rucker 5.0 refines the 4.0 where you feel it most. GORUCK kept the dedicated plate pockets, the 1000D CORDURA shell, the frame sheet and the lifetime guarantee, and changed the two things you touch most: the lumbar pad against your lower back and the closures on the internal pockets.

I've carried the 25L Rucker 5.0 with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, and my full Rucker 5.0 review covers that in detail. I haven't carried a 4.0, so the older bag's side of this comparison comes from GORUCK's published information. GORUCK supplied my 5.0 for review.

What changed from the 4.0 to the 5.0

GORUCK released the 5.0 on May 8, 2026. For the full launch breakdown, including why the magnetic closures matter at pre-dawn training and events, see our Rucker 5.0 launch analysis.

What the changes felt like on my back

The lumbar pad is the upgrade you'd feel

On the table, the 5.0's lumbar pad looks enormous. On my back, it felt supportive and soon faded into the background, and I had no lower-back discomfort on the filmed hike.

The reason a bigger, contoured pad matters is the curve of your lower back. A pad that fills more of that hollow gives the pack a firmer second point of contact below your shoulder blades, so the bottom of the pack has less room to rock with each step. That second contact probably did as much for the 5.0's stability on rough ground as the plate pocket did.

So the upgrade question mostly comes down to your 4.0's lumbar pad. If you finish a ruck with a sore or rubbed lower back, or you feel the bottom of the pack moving, that's the problem the 5.0 was built to fix. If you never think about your lower back in a 4.0, you already have most of what the 5.0 offers.

The magnetic pockets are a quality-of-life change

I kept my keys in a magnetic internal pocket, and it was simply easy: one hand, no rip of hook-and-loop. Magnets are quiet and don't clog with lint, sand or dog hair the way hook-and-loop does over years of use. They're a genuine quality-of-life upgrade, and if your 4.0's pockets have worn out and stopped holding, they count for even more.

What didn't change

The plate still rides high and tight against your back, which is why both generations carry better than an everyday bag with a plate sleeve. With a 20 lb plate, the 5.0 stayed stable where I had to work to keep my own balance. Strap setup, the sternum strap and the optional hip belt work the same way in both generations, and my full review goes into what I'd change for heavier loads.

Should you upgrade from a Rucker 4.0?

Upgrade to the 5.0 if:

Your lower back gets sore, rubbed or hot in the 4.0, or you feel the bottom of the pack rocking.

The hook-and-loop on your 4.0's internal pockets has worn out and no longer holds.

You're moving up to heavier plates, where lumbar contact and stability matter more.

You've been meaning to change sizes anyway.

Keep your 4.0 if:

It fits well and you finish rucks without lower-back complaints.

Most of your frustration is shoulder soreness. The lumbar pad won't fix that; a snug sternum strap, a hip belt or building up the weight more gradually will do more. Our rucking form guide covers strap setup.

You'd rather put the money toward a heavier plate or better shoes.

GORUCK hasn't announced a way to retrofit the new lumbar pad or magnetic closures to a 4.0, so upgrading means replacing the bag.

Buying your first Rucker

If you don't own either, I'd buy the 5.0. It's the version I've carried, and the lumbar pad is the change you'd notice on every ruck. A discounted 4.0 is still a proper dedicated ruck with the same plate-first design, so if you find one on sale and you'd rather keep the difference, it's a sensible buy. If you're only starting out with light loads, GORUCK's cheaper Basic Rucker is worth a look first, and our best rucking backpacks guide compares the field beyond GORUCK.

Deciding between a Rucker and GORUCK's everyday bags instead? I've carried all three with the same 20 lb plate, and my GR1 review and GR2 review compare them directly.

Frequently asked questions

The Rucker 5.0 adds a larger, contoured lumbar pad of 30mm high-density foam in place of the 4.0's 20mm pad, and magnetic closures on the internal pockets in place of hook-and-loop. The shell, frame sheet, elevated plate storage, carry handles and lifetime guarantee carry over.

Yes, mainly because of the lumbar pad. In my own use of the 5.0, the pad felt supportive and the pack stayed stable on rough ground. If a 4.0 already fits you comfortably, the difference is smaller.

Yes, especially at a discount. It's still a dedicated rucking pack that holds the plate high, with the same construction and guarantee. You give up the newer lumbar pad and magnetic pockets.

GORUCK released the Rucker 5.0 on May 8, 2026.

GORUCK hasn't announced a retrofit. The lumbar pad and magnetic closures are built into the 5.0, so getting them means buying the new bag.

